# Frozen numeric ABI for ContractError (Issue #394).
#
# Off-chain consumers — the dashboard, the indexer, the action runner — read
# these numbers out of a failed invocation's XDR result. A variant that gets
# renumbered silently re-labels every historical failure those consumers have
# already recorded, so the mapping is append-only and this file is the record.
#
# Format:
#   <code> <Variant>   a live code, frozen to that variant
#   !<code> reserved   a code that must stay unclaimed (see below)
#   # ...              comment
#
# Adding an error: append the next unused code here and to the enum. Never
# reuse, reorder, or renumber an existing one.

1 AlreadyInitialized
2 NotInitialized
3 NotAuthorized
4 NotRegistered
5 AlreadyVerified
6 NotVerified
7 Paused
8 CooldownActive
9 InvalidVersion
10 InvalidRole
11 InvalidUsername
12 AttestationExpired
13 UnattestedWasm
14 InvalidBatchSize
15 InvalidReasonCode
16 ZeroAddress
17 ChallengeAlreadyActive
18 NoChallengeActive
19 ChallengeNotResolvable
20 ChallengeActive
21 InvalidPauseReason
22 AlreadyReserved
23 NotReserved
24 UsernameReserved
25 ReservedListFull
26 AdminTransferPending
27 AdminTransferDelayActive
28 NoPendingAdminTransfer
29 AttestationRequired

# Code 30 was skipped when the verifier-allowlist errors (31-33) were added.
# It is recorded as reserved rather than filled: handing it to a new error now
# would make 30 mean one thing in this build and nothing in every earlier one,
# which is the same ambiguity renumbering causes. The test asserts that
# `from_code(30)` stays `None`.
!30 reserved

31 VerifierAllowlistFull
32 VerifierNotAllowlisted
33 VerifierExpiryInPast
34 NoPendingRoleGrant
35 RoleGrantNotReady
36 ProvenanceMissing
37 ProvenanceMismatch
57 RoleExpired
